---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const PRICE = "$PRICE";

// The refund window and its conditions are the owner's to set. Nothing is
// invented here: until a policy is written, the page says plainly that one has
// not been published yet and names the route to ask.
const REFUND_POLICY = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_FA_REFUND_POLICY ?? "";
const SUPPORT_EMAIL = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_FA_SUPPORT_EMAIL ?? "";
---

<Base
  title="Festival Activity terms of sale"
  description="What you buy when you buy Festival Activity, and the three limits worth knowing before you do."
>
  <section class="inner-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">FESTIVAL ACTIVITY</p>
    <h1>Terms of sale.</h1>
    <p>
      Every sentence here describes something the software actually does. The
      three limits that would be easier to leave out are in sections three,
      four, and five.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="work-case fa-terms">
    <div class="fa-terms-body">
      <h2>1. What you are buying</h2>
      <p>
        A license to use Festival Activity version 1 on up to two of your own
        installations, for as long as you like. The price is {PRICE}, paid once.
        There is no subscription and nothing renews on your card.
      </p>

      <h2>2. How activation works</h2>
      <p>
        You paste your key into the app once. The app exchanges it for a lease
        that lasts 90 days and is tied to that installation. While the app can
        reach the internet it renews the lease on its own, before it runs out.
      </p>
      <p>
        Between renewals the app needs no network at all. You can use it with no
        internet connection for the whole 90 days, because it checks the lease
        on your own machine rather than asking us.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Installations, and what one is</h2>
      <p>
        Two at a time. You can release one from inside the app, or ask us to
        release it for you.
      </p>
      <p>
        An installation is a copy of the app on a machine, identified by a
        random id stored alongside your data. It is not a fingerprint of your
        hardware. Reinstalling the app keeps the same installation and the same
        seat. Reinstalling your operating system, restoring to a new machine, or
        moving to a different computer creates a new installation and uses one
        of your two.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is a real limit and we would rather say it than dress it up.
        Hardware fingerprinting would break for anyone who replaced a component
        and would promise something this product does not do. If you run out of
        seats, ask and one will be freed.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Refunds, and what a refund does not do</h2>
      <p>
        {
          REFUND_POLICY ? (
            REFUND_POLICY
          ) : (
            <>
              A refund policy has not been published yet. Until it is, ask
              {SUPPORT_EMAIL ? (
                <>
                  {" "}
                  at <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>
                </>
              ) : (
                <>
                  {" "}
                  through the <a href="/#contact">contact form</a>
                </>
              )}{" "}
              and you will get a straight answer.
            </>
          )
        }
      </p>
      <p>
        Whatever the policy, one thing is true of every refund and every
        revocation: any lease already issued keeps working until it expires. The
        app checks your license on your own machine with no network, so revoking
        a license stops new activations and renewals and reaches no installation
        that already holds a lease. In the worst case that is 90 days of working
        software after a refund. We would rather tell you that than pretend
        otherwise.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Lost keys</h2>
      <p>
        {
          SUPPORT_EMAIL ? (
            <>
              Write to <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>
            </>
          ) : (
            <>
              Write through the <a href="/#contact">contact form</a>
            </>
          )
        } with the email address you paid with. A person reads it and answers. There
        is no automated recovery and no self service portal, because this is one product
        at one price and an automated recovery flow would be a second system to secure.
      </p>

      <h2>6. Your data</h2>
      <p>
        The timeline is built on your machine, from your own repositories, and
        stays in <code>~/.obey/plugins/camp-timeline</code>. Nothing about your
        work is sent anywhere. We never see your repository names, your commit
        messages, your file paths, or your timeline.
      </p>
      <p>
        The only network requests the app makes are activating, renewing, and
        deactivating your license. Those carry your license key, your
        installation id, the platform you are on, and the app version, and
        nothing else.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Updates</h2>
      <p>
        Every version 1 update is included, for as long as version 1 is
        maintained. A future major version is a separate purchase, and your
        version 1 license keeps working when it arrives.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Requirements</h2>
      <p>
        macOS 13 or later on Apple silicon or Intel, and Linux on x86_64. There
        is no Windows build. Camp and Fest are installed separately and are
        free, and <code>git</code> must be on your PATH.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Who you are buying from</h2>
      <p>
        Obedience Corp. Payment is taken by Stripe, which handles your card
        details; we never see them. Tax is calculated and collected at checkout
        where we are registered to collect it.
      </p>

      <p class="fa-terms-back">
        <a href="/festival-activity">Back to Festival Activity</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .fa-terms {
    background: none;
  }

  .fa-terms-body {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .fa-terms-body h2 {
    margin: 3rem 0 0.9rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 1.2vw + 1.05rem, 1.75rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }

  .fa-terms-body h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .fa-terms-body p {
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.68;
  }

  .fa-terms-body code {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.92em;
  }

  .fa-terms-back {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  }
</style>
